            question: 'Define Price Elasticity of Demand (PED) and explain its formula. Why is PED always negative and why do we use absolute values?',
            answer: 'Price Elasticity of Demand (PED) measures the responsiveness of quantity demanded to a change in price. Formula: PED = (% change in quantity demanded) / (% change in price) = [(ΔQd/Qd) × 100] / [(ΔP/P) × 100] = (ΔQd/Qd) / (ΔP/P). PED is always negative because of the Law of Demand: price and quantity demanded move in opposite directions (inverse relationship). When price increases, quantity demanded decreases (negative ΔQd, positive ΔP), giving negative PED. When price decreases, quantity demanded increases (positive ΔQd, negative ΔP), also giving negative PED. We use absolute values |PED| when classifying elasticity to focus on the magnitude of responsiveness rather than direction. For example, if price increases by 10% and quantity demanded falls by 20%, PED = -20%/10% = -2, or |PED| = 2 (elastic demand). The absolute value of 2 indicates high responsiveness, while the negative sign simply confirms the inverse relationship.',
